1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,223
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,243 --> 00:00:04,815
and welcome to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:05,215 --> 00:00:08,313
This is Lecture 31, the last one in the course,

4
00:00:08,333 --> 00:00:11,299
on artificial intelligence in apparel making.

5
00:00:11,699 --> 00:00:14,650
Last time we looked ten years ahead at the machines.

6
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:18,249
Today we look at AI and ask it three things: what data it eats,

7
00:00:18,269 --> 00:00:20,592
how it's judged, and where it goes wrong.

8
00:00:20,992 --> 00:00:25,171
This video concentrates on whether a vision inspector's alarms can be trusted

9
00:00:25,191 --> 00:00:27,788
and on the trade-offs in predictive maintenance;

10
00:00:27,808 --> 00:00:30,788
design, marker making and jobs get only a mention here,

11
00:00:30,808 --> 00:00:32,478
with the details in the slides.

12
00:00:33,998 --> 00:00:34,968
Here's the problem.

13
00:00:35,368 --> 00:00:39,527
A shirt factory plans to install vision inspection on the collar line.

14
00:00:39,927 --> 00:00:44,263
The supplier's report says recall 95 percent, false-alarm rate 3 percent.

15
00:00:44,663 --> 00:00:47,081
The station's defect rate is 2 percent;

16
00:00:47,101 --> 00:00:51,955
a missed defect reaching the next station or the customer costs about 20 yuan,

17
00:00:51,975 --> 00:00:55,580
and a false alarm costs about 0.5 yuan to recheck by hand.

18
00:00:55,980 --> 00:01:00,547
Out of 1000 pieces, how many alarms, and what share are real defects?

19
00:01:00,947 --> 00:01:03,443
Where should the threshold go to minimise cost?

20
00:01:03,843 --> 00:01:07,177
And to reach 80 percent precision with 90 percent recall,

21
00:01:07,197 --> 00:01:09,472
how much better must the inspector get?

22
00:01:09,872 --> 00:01:11,272
We'll answer at the end.

23
00:01:12,792 --> 00:01:14,740
What AI does in apparel making.

24
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:19,516
By what they do, AI applications in apparel fall into four kinds.

25
00:01:19,916 --> 00:01:23,038
Perception: recognising things in images and signals,

26
00:01:23,058 --> 00:01:27,010
such as vision inspection, fabric identification and edge tracking;

27
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:30,982
its weakness is the unseen: a new colour or style and it misjudges.

28
00:01:31,382 --> 00:01:34,328
Prediction: projecting the past forward, such as sales,

29
00:01:34,348 --> 00:01:37,618
delays and machine failures; its weakness is a changed world.

30
00:01:38,018 --> 00:01:41,788
Optimisation and generation: finding good options or making new ones,

31
00:01:41,808 --> 00:01:44,260
such as markers, schedules and design images;

32
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:46,347
its weakness is a wrongly stated goal.

33
00:01:46,747 --> 00:01:49,589
Language assistants: reading documents and answering questions;

34
00:01:49,609 --> 00:01:51,950
their weakness is sounding fluent while being wrong.

35
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:56,830
For any AI application, ask three things: what data, how it's judged,

36
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:58,069
and where it fails.

37
00:01:59,589 --> 00:02:01,694
Vision inspection: get three ratios straight.

38
00:02:03,214 --> 00:02:07,000
Inspection results fit a four-cell table, the confusion matrix.

39
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,411
With the supplier's figures,

40
00:02:09,431 --> 00:02:14,777
out of 1000 pieces the 20 defects split into about 19 caught and 1 missed,

41
00:02:14,797 --> 00:02:20,289
and the 980 good pieces into about 29 false alarms and 951 correctly passed.

42
00:02:20,689 --> 00:02:21,878
Keep three ratios apart.

43
00:02:22,278 --> 00:02:24,475
Recall is the share of defects caught,

44
00:02:24,495 --> 00:02:28,032
and the false-alarm rate is the share of good pieces flagged;

45
00:02:28,052 --> 00:02:30,015
these two belong to the inspector.

46
00:02:30,415 --> 00:02:33,407
Precision is the share of alarms that are real defects,

47
00:02:33,427 --> 00:02:35,543
and it also depends on the defect rate.

48
00:02:37,063 --> 00:02:39,315
The inspector gives each piece a 'looks defective' score.

49
00:02:39,715 --> 00:02:44,349
Good pieces score as a normal distribution with mean 0 and standard deviation 1;

50
00:02:44,369 --> 00:02:48,537
defects score with mean d-prime, the discriminability: the larger it is,

51
00:02:48,557 --> 00:02:50,863
the further apart the two distributions.

52
00:02:51,263 --> 00:02:54,037
A score above threshold t raises an alarm.

53
00:02:54,437 --> 00:02:57,283
Recall is one minus Phi of t minus d-prime;

54
00:02:57,303 --> 00:03:00,150
the false-alarm rate is one minus Phi of t.

55
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:02,768
Watch the threshold sweep in the lab:

56
00:03:02,788 --> 00:03:05,914
move it right and false alarms fall but misses rise;

57
00:03:05,934 --> 00:03:07,729
move it left and the opposite.

58
00:03:08,129 --> 00:03:09,266
In our example,

59
00:03:09,286 --> 00:03:15,593
d-prime 3.53 and threshold 1.88 give 95 percent recall and 3 percent false alarms.

60
00:03:17,113 --> 00:03:19,829
Here's the thing most often missed: the base rate.

61
00:03:20,229 --> 00:03:24,899
At a 2 percent defect rate, 1000 pieces bring about 48 alarms,

62
00:03:24,919 --> 00:03:29,514
only about 19 of them real, so precision is about 39 percent:

63
00:03:29,534 --> 00:03:31,633
six alarms in ten are false.

64
00:03:32,033 --> 00:03:37,192
The same inspector at a 10 percent defect rate has about 78 percent precision,

65
00:03:37,212 --> 00:03:39,250
and at 1 percent only about 24.

66
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:42,555
So don't look at 'accuracy' alone: at 2 percent defects,

67
00:03:42,575 --> 00:03:46,732
an inspector that always says 'good' is 98 percent accurate and catches nothing.

68
00:03:48,252 --> 00:03:52,827
Join up recall and false-alarm rate for every threshold and you get the ROC

69
00:03:52,847 --> 00:03:53,929
curve on the left;

70
00:03:53,949 --> 00:03:57,912
the closer it hugs the top-left corner, the better the inspector.

71
00:03:58,312 --> 00:04:01,867
On the right is cost per 1000 pieces against threshold:

72
00:04:01,887 --> 00:04:06,939
misses times the cost of a miss plus false alarms times the cost of a recheck.

73
00:04:07,339 --> 00:04:10,767
Too low a threshold means many false alarms and high cost;

74
00:04:10,787 --> 00:04:15,167
too high means many misses and high cost too; there's a valley in between.

75
00:04:15,567 --> 00:04:19,512
Watch the dots move with the threshold: sliding along the ROC curve,

76
00:04:19,532 --> 00:04:21,553
and down then up on the cost curve.

77
00:04:23,073 --> 00:04:25,350
The cheapest threshold can be computed directly.

78
00:04:25,749 --> 00:04:30,281
Under the equal-variance normal model, t-star equals d-prime over 2,

79
00:04:30,301 --> 00:04:34,632
plus the natural log of one minus pi times C_f over pi times C_m,

80
00:04:34,652 --> 00:04:35,903
divided by d-prime.

81
00:04:36,303 --> 00:04:40,934
At a 2 percent defect rate, 20 yuan per miss and 0.5 per recheck,

82
00:04:40,954 --> 00:04:45,513
t-star is about 1.82 and the cost about 34 yuan per 1000 pieces,

83
00:04:45,533 --> 00:04:48,517
slightly cheaper than the supplier's 1.88.

84
00:04:48,917 --> 00:04:53,407
At 10 percent defects the cheapest threshold falls to about 1.34;

85
00:04:53,427 --> 00:04:55,905
at 1 percent it rises to about 2.02.

86
00:04:56,305 --> 00:04:59,493
The rarer the defects or the dearer the false alarms,

87
00:04:59,513 --> 00:05:01,611
the higher the threshold should go.

88
00:05:03,131 --> 00:05:04,549
Predictive maintenance.

89
00:05:06,069 --> 00:05:09,484
The second classic application is predictive maintenance.

90
00:05:09,884 --> 00:05:15,102
In our example, 200 machines over 180 days each report one indicator a day,

91
00:05:15,122 --> 00:05:20,409
such as the RMS of vibration or motor current, normalised to 1 when healthy,

92
00:05:20,429 --> 00:05:24,600
with noise of 0.08; 89 of them start to degrade on some day,

93
00:05:24,620 --> 00:05:28,231
growing exponentially until they fail 30 days later.

94
00:05:28,631 --> 00:05:32,463
Look at three machines: green is healthy, orange and blue degrade.

95
00:05:32,863 --> 00:05:37,442
The thin line is the daily value and the thick line the moving average,

96
00:05:37,462 --> 00:05:40,227
cycling through windows of 1, 3 and 7 days.

97
00:05:40,627 --> 00:05:41,908
The longer the window,

98
00:05:41,928 --> 00:05:45,573
the smoother the line and the fewer noise-driven false alarms,

99
00:05:45,593 --> 00:05:49,297
but the later it crosses the threshold once degradation starts.

100
00:05:50,817 --> 00:05:53,021
AI sewing machines and make-to-order.

101
00:05:54,541 --> 00:05:59,288
Parts of a garment are one ply, parts four or five, like a jeans yoke crossing.

102
00:05:59,688 --> 00:06:03,068
A traditional lockstitch machine keeps its stitch length,

103
00:06:03,088 --> 00:06:05,514
tension and foot pressure fixed once set,

104
00:06:05,534 --> 00:06:10,049
so sudden thickness changes cause uneven stitches, skips and broken needles.

105
00:06:10,449 --> 00:06:12,747
An AI sewing machine closes a loop.

106
00:06:13,147 --> 00:06:17,714
Sense: foot height for thickness, spindle current for penetration resistance,

107
00:06:17,734 --> 00:06:19,859
feed-motor load for feed resistance.

108
00:06:20,259 --> 00:06:23,921
Recognise: more plies, a cross seam, or a different fabric.

109
00:06:24,321 --> 00:06:28,299
Adjust: electronic feed, tension and foot carry out the change,

110
00:06:28,319 --> 00:06:29,759
then sensing continues.

111
00:06:30,159 --> 00:06:33,470
A published patent's example simply measures thickness at two moments and

112
00:06:33,490 --> 00:06:34,246
looks up a table.

113
00:06:35,766 --> 00:06:37,567
The same trade-offs apply to robots.

114
00:06:37,967 --> 00:06:42,526
In bin picking, vision scores each candidate grasp point with a confidence,

115
00:06:42,546 --> 00:06:45,212
and the robot grasps only above a threshold:

116
00:06:45,232 --> 00:06:49,912
too low and it grabs air or the wrong part, too high and it skips good parts.

117
00:06:50,312 --> 00:06:54,033
That's the same ROC curve as inspection recall and false alarms,

118
00:06:54,053 --> 00:06:58,766
and the threshold should be set by the cost of a wrong grasp versus a missed one.

119
00:06:59,166 --> 00:07:01,145
When a cobot's joint reducer wears,

120
00:07:01,165 --> 00:07:03,829
motor current under the same load slowly rises;

121
00:07:03,849 --> 00:07:08,112
average it and compare with a threshold, and that's predictive maintenance.

122
00:07:08,512 --> 00:07:11,465
Force-controlled grinding mirrors the AI sewing machine's

123
00:07:11,485 --> 00:07:12,926
sense-recognise-adjust loop,

124
00:07:12,946 --> 00:07:14,595
with limits to prevent overload.

125
00:07:16,115 --> 00:07:17,862
The same thing happens at home.

126
00:07:18,262 --> 00:07:22,332
The kitchen smoke alarm goes off every time someone fries something;

127
00:07:22,352 --> 00:07:25,880
ten alarms and not one fire, so somebody pulls the battery,

128
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:27,985
and in a real fire it stays silent.

129
00:07:28,385 --> 00:07:32,219
When alarm precision is too low, people stop believing alarms.

130
00:07:32,619 --> 00:07:37,495
Vision inspection on the floor is no different: if six alarms in ten are false,

131
00:07:37,515 --> 00:07:40,036
operators soon stop rechecking carefully.

132
00:07:40,436 --> 00:07:43,827
That's exactly why inspection thresholds must be set by cost,

133
00:07:43,847 --> 00:07:45,896
and why precision must be calculated.

134
00:07:47,416 --> 00:07:48,657
Back to our problem.

135
00:07:49,057 --> 00:07:53,104
Of 1000 pieces, about 19 of the 20 defects are caught,

136
00:07:53,124 --> 00:07:57,548
and about 29 of the 980 good pieces are flagged by mistake:

137
00:07:57,568 --> 00:08:01,766
about 48 alarms in all, with precision about 39 percent,

138
00:08:01,786 --> 00:08:03,574
six alarms in ten false.

139
00:08:03,974 --> 00:08:08,353
By the cost formula the cheapest threshold is about 1.82,

140
00:08:08,373 --> 00:08:11,285
costing about 34 yuan per 1000 pieces.

141
00:08:11,685 --> 00:08:16,703
For 80 percent precision with 90 percent recall no threshold will do;

142
00:08:16,723 --> 00:08:19,696
d-prime must rise from 3.53 to about 3.9.

143
00:08:20,096 --> 00:08:21,959
So '95 percent recall' can be believed,

144
00:08:21,979 --> 00:08:23,938
but it doesn't mean the alarms are right.

145
00:08:25,458 --> 00:08:30,076
Now Virtual lab 31-1, AI inspection and predictive maintenance. The results,

146
00:08:30,096 --> 00:08:33,432
the confusion-matrix numbers and the tasks are blurred.

147
00:08:33,452 --> 00:08:37,704
Page one is the inspection threshold. Record recall, false-alarm rate,

148
00:08:37,724 --> 00:08:40,816
precision and cost per 1000 pieces at the defaults,

149
00:08:40,836 --> 00:08:45,332
then drag the alarm threshold and watch the line on the score plot and the

150
00:08:45,352 --> 00:08:48,139
dots on the ROC and cost curves move together.

151
00:08:48,539 --> 00:08:51,726
Next, change the cost of a miss and of a false alarm,

152
00:08:51,746 --> 00:08:54,691
see which way the bottom of the cost curve moves,

153
00:08:54,711 --> 00:08:56,930
and check it with the t-star formula.

154
00:08:56,950 --> 00:09:00,682
Task one asks for the cheapest threshold at the default costs.

155
00:09:01,082 --> 00:09:05,708
Task two puts the threshold back at 1.88 and sets the defect rate to 10 and

156
00:09:05,728 --> 00:09:06,637
then 1 percent;

157
00:09:06,657 --> 00:09:11,221
record precision and the alarm count. The inspector hasn't changed at all,

158
00:09:11,241 --> 00:09:14,009
yet its alarms mean something very different.

159
00:09:14,409 --> 00:09:18,801
Task three keeps 2 percent defects and raises the discriminability d-prime

160
00:09:18,821 --> 00:09:19,576
step by step,

161
00:09:19,596 --> 00:09:20,947
retuning the threshold,

162
00:09:20,967 --> 00:09:25,836
to find the smallest d-prime that meets the precision and recall targets together.

163
00:09:26,236 --> 00:09:29,343
Page two is predictive maintenance. Record detection rate,

164
00:09:29,363 --> 00:09:33,170
average lead time and false alarms per machine a month at the defaults,

165
00:09:33,190 --> 00:09:37,213
then drag the alarm threshold and watch the triangles on the three machines

166
00:09:37,233 --> 00:09:37,860
come and go.

167
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:42,953
Task four keeps the noise at 0.08 and combines the moving-average window and

168
00:09:42,973 --> 00:09:44,565
threshold until detection,

169
00:09:44,585 --> 00:09:47,541
false alarms and lead time all meet the targets;

170
00:09:47,561 --> 00:09:49,711
then try a long window and compare.

